  "query_preview": "..Broad high pressure over the desert southwest with\nmid-level flow turning to the southeast this afternoon and to the\nsouth tonight as the upper high moves into SW NM. Higher up around\n300-200 mb, the flow will be westerly that becomes divergent along\nthe Intl border this afternoon and evening. PWATs range from 1.25\"\nnear AZ/NM border to 1.70\" across western Pima county. SE AZ will\nsee much more thunderstorm activity today versus yesterday with\nthe most recent runs from the HRRR, RRFS and HREF all highlighting\nSW Cochise county and most of Santa Cruz county as the area for\nconcern today from scattered to numerous thunderstorms. The main\nthreat in this area will be very heavy rain with some of the \nstrongest cores producing rainfall rates of 1\"-2.5\"/hr. HREF has\nthis area painted with 10+% chance for 3-hour rainfall totals \nexceeding 3+\". WPCs ERO has this area under slight risk, at \nleast 15% chance, for excessive rain leading to flash flooding. \nAlso, parts of eastern Santa Cruz county and SW Cochise county \nhas seen heavy rain over the past 72 hours, so the soils are \nwet, and the San Pedro has had some increased flow several days \nago. With that said I contemplated hoisting a small flash flood \nwatch area between I-19 in Santa Cruz county east to Sierra \nVista area. Will let day shift look at new model data in case \none needs to be issued.\n\nSaturday: Might be a down day depending how today evolves.\n\nSunday through Thursday: Southerly flow aloft will keep slightly\nabove normal moisture across the area for mid-September \nresulting in a chance of showers and thunderstorms with day-to- \nday variability in areal coverage of storms, mainly across the \neastern half of the forecast area. \n\nHigh temperatures through Monday will be at or slightly above\nnormal then be at or slightly below normal Tuesday through\nThursday.",
